				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=617958&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606921229" alt="Communism will win" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=617958)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;" >Communism will win</a></span> <span class="odate time_1472786982 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">02 Sep 2016 03:29</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2583710">
									

<p>It's about something having a crush on the Foundation (and the question of what <em>could</em> see the Foundation in this way), and it's about the Foundation trying out cool weird technologies. In-universe there's a perfectly good reason that these two things are presented together, but as a story it's disjointed.</p>
<p>The thing's diction is nothing special, and to be honest I kind of glazed over the specifics. I don't enjoy reading love notes, much less when they're some long-winded pompous-sounding bs. And on an equally personal level, I'm getting bored of articles whose main thrust is in notes, diaries, broadcasts, etc. and this article is skimpy as hell without them.</p>
<p>I literally can't read the containment procedures, and I doubt many people who <em>can</em> are interested in choking down a fifteen-line paragraph of minutia for handling letters. From what I can glean, they seem to be exceptionally detailed, which might make sense in-universe but is utterly unwarranted for the story, considering that they're mostly a formality anyways. Cut it down to two paragraphs, maybe ten lines total, and shove some of the details off into a nonexistent supplemental document.</p>
<p>For what I did manage to get out of this article, it felt like a worse version of <a href="/scp-962">SCP-962</a>, which has (some of) the same themes, executed much more memorably. I've remembered almost everything about 962 for years now, but there's nothing about 2371 that I'll remember next week.</p>

							</div>

